---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * The Chrome Web Store requires a privacy policy URL, and this is it. The
 * store also makes the publisher certify that the policy matches the
 * disclosures on the listing — so the claims here are generated from
 * src/data/legal.ts, which is the same file the listing copy is checked
 * against. They cannot drift apart without someone editing the shared source.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { extensionDataFacts, legal, security, updatedLabel } from "../../data/legal";
import { CONSENT_CATEGORIES } from "../../data/consent";
import { toddleExtension } from "../../data/site";

const title = "Privacy policy — Nyuchi Learning";
const description =
  "What the Toddle Enhancement Extension and learning.nyuchi.com do with data. In short: the extension sends nothing anywhere on its own, only feedback you choose to send; the website asks before it measures anything, and lets you take all, some or none of it.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical="/legal/privacy">
  <article class="container-prose section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Legal</p>
    <h1 class="font-serif text-h1 text-foreground text-balance">Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body-sm text-muted-foreground">
      Last updated {updatedLabel}. Applies to
      <strong class="text-foreground">learning.nyuchi.com</strong> and to the
      <strong class="text-foreground">{toddleExtension.name}</strong>.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10 ubuntu-quote">
      <p class="text-body text-pretty">
        <strong>The short version.</strong> The extension reads Toddle inside your own
        browser, for you at the screen, and sends it nowhere. There is no server, no account,
        no analytics and no tracking — buying a licence does not change that, because your
        key is checked on your own machine. The one thing the extension will ever send is
        feedback you write in its menu and choose to send, and that never includes anything
        from Toddle. We do hold your email address if you bought something, the
        way any shop does. This website asks before it measures anything and lets you take
        all, some or none of it; the extension measures nothing at all, and never will.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Who we are</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      {legal.entity}, trading as Nyuchi Learning, registered in {legal.country}.
      For anything in this policy, write to
      <a
        href={`mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`}
        class="link"
        >{legal.privacyEmail}</a
      >.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">The extension</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      Each of the following is a statement about how the software is built, not a promise
      about how we intend to behave. They can be checked against the extension itself.
    </p>

    <dl class="mt-6 space-y-6">
      {
        extensionDataFacts.map((fact) => (
          <div class="card p-6">
            <dt class="font-serif text-h5 text-foreground">{fact.claim}</dt>
            <dd class="mt-2 text-body-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">{fact.detail}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <h2 id="extension-feedback" class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12 scroll-mt-24">
      The extension's feedback form
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      This is the one exception to "sends nothing", and it only happens when you make it
      happen. Since version 0.8.1 the extension's toolbar menu has a
      <strong class="text-foreground">Send feedback</strong> form.
    </p>
    <ul
      class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-body text-muted-foreground list-disc list-outside pl-5 marker:text-foreground/40"
    >
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">When.</strong> Only when you press Send. Opening
        the form, typing in it or closing it sends nothing, and nothing is ever sent in the
        background.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">What.</strong> Exactly what you typed — the topic,
        your message, and your email address only if you enter one — plus the extension's
        version number, so we know which release you mean.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">What it never includes.</strong> Nothing from
        Toddle: no student data, no page address, no class or school details, and not your
        licence key. Please leave student names out of what you type, too.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">Where it goes.</strong> To Nyuchi's feedback form,
        which is processed by Formspree, a form service acting on our behalf. It is the
        same service and the same form as this website's
        <a class="link" href="/feedback">feedback page</a>, and it is handled the same way
        once it reaches us.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">Why.</strong> To read what you tell us, reply if
        you asked us to, and decide what to fix or build next. We do not add you to a
        mailing list or share it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">How long.</strong> Feedback is kept only as long as
        we need it to act on it. You can ask for yours to be deleted at any time by writing
        to
        <a class="link" href={`mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`}>{legal.privacyEmail}</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="security" class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12 scroll-mt-24">
      Security
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      The statements above are enforced in the software and held in place by tests that
      fail if the extension asks for a new permission, sends anything outside Toddle, or
      sends a query that is not on its fixed, read-only list. On {security.review.label}
      it had an independent adversarial review, and every finding was fixed in version
      {security.version}. The <a class="link" href="/legal/security">Security page</a>
      sets out the security model, how it is tested, what that review found, the limits
      no extension can remove, and how to report a vulnerability.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">
      Are you a processor of our students' data?
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      No. A data processor receives personal data and does something with it on the
      controller's behalf. We receive none: the extension reads what your browser has
      already loaded from Toddle, renders it differently on that same screen, and
      transmits none of it. Feedback a teacher chooses to send from the extension's menu
      carries only what they typed, never anything from Toddle. Your school remains the controller of its data, and Toddle
      remains its processor, exactly as before the extension was installed.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      If your data protection officer would like that in writing against a specific
      framework, or wants to review the code before approving it, write to us — we would
      rather answer the question properly than have you take a web page's word for it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">This website</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      It is a set of static pages served by Vercel. Vercel processes the request in order
      to serve it and keeps operational logs, as any host must.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      If you use the <a class="link" href="/feedback">feedback form</a> on this website,
      what you type —
      the topic, your message, your email address if you give one, and the page you came
      from — is sent through Formspree, a form service, and recorded as an issue in
      {legal.shortEntity}'s private GitHub repository for the extension, where only our
      team can see it. We use it to reply to you and to decide what to build; we do not
      add you to a mailing list or share it. Feedback is kept only as long as we need it to
      act on it; ask us at
      <a class="link" href={`mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`}>{legal.privacyEmail}</a> and
      we will delete it sooner. Feedback sent from inside the extension goes to the same
      form — see <a class="link" href="#extension-feedback">the extension's feedback form</a>.
    </p>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Your choices, and what they control</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      The first time you visit, a banner asks. Nothing optional is switched on until you
      switch it on, and you can take all of it, some of it, or none. Ignoring the banner
      leaves you exactly where refusing does.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-5">
      {
        CONSENT_CATEGORIES.map((category) => (
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-serif text-h4 text-foreground">
              {category.name}
              {category.required && (
                <span class="text-body-sm text-muted-foreground font-sans"> · always on</span>
              )}
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-body-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
              {category.detail}
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1 text-body-sm text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
              {category.used}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="mt-6 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      <strong class="text-foreground">Changing your mind costs one press.</strong>
      <strong class="text-foreground">Cookie choices</strong> at the bottom of any page reopens
      the panel with your current answers, and withdrawing is as easy as agreeing was. We keep
      a record of what you chose and when, because the law puts the burden of showing that on
      us rather than on you — it is stored in your own browser, alongside the choice itself.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      This is written to satisfy the three regimes that actually apply to our readers:
      <strong class="text-foreground">UK and EU GDPR</strong>, Zimbabwe's
      <strong class="text-foreground">Data Protection Act (Chapter 11:12)</strong>, and
      Singapore's <strong class="text-foreground">PDPA</strong>. They differ in detail and
      agree on the substance, so one approach serves all three: nothing optional before you
      ask for it, each purpose chosen separately, refusal as easy as agreement, withdrawal at
      any time, and a record we can produce.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      One detail we would rather state than have you discover. While consent is denied,
      Google's tag is still on the page and still contacts Google, without cookies and
      without identifying you — that is how Google's consent mechanism is built. So "no
      cookies until you agree" is exactly true; "no contact with Google until you agree"
      would not be, and we are not going to claim it.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      <strong class="text-foreground"
        >None of that is true of the extension.</strong
      > It contains no analytics and sends nothing anywhere unless you press Send on its
      feedback form. This is the website measuring
      the website. If the distinction matters to you — and for anyone assessing the
      software for a school, it is the part that matters — the extension section above is
      the one to read.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      Fonts are loaded from Google Fonts, so your browser fetches them from Google and
      Google sees your IP address in doing so.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      <strong class="text-foreground">The support messenger is opt-in.</strong> There is a
      Support button in the corner of every page. Until you press it Intercom loads nothing
      and sets no cookie of its own — the analytics above is separate, and arrives without
      being asked. Press it and the messenger loads from
      <a href={legal.helpCentre} rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">Intercom</a>, which
      at that point sets its own cookies, can see which pages you have open while you use
      it, and holds the conversation on our behalf so we can answer you. We chose to load it
      on demand rather than on arrival precisely so that reading this site costs you
      nothing.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">If you buy a licence</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      This is the one place we hold personal data about you, and it is worth being exact
      about it, because it is easy to confuse with what the extension does.
    </p>
    <ul
      class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-body text-muted-foreground list-disc list-outside pl-5 marker:text-foreground/40"
    >
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">We hold your email address and your order.</strong>
        We need them to issue your key, to let you renew, and to give you an invoice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">Your payment goes to Buy Me a Coffee, not to us.</strong>
        It processes the order, with Stripe handling the card, and passes us your email
        address and what you bought so we can issue your key. We never see your card
        details.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">Your key contains your email.</strong> That is how
        a key is tied to a person. It is stored in your own browser and checked there —
        the extension does not send it to us or to anyone else.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-foreground">We do not market to you off the back of it.</strong>
        Buying a licence does not put you on a mailing list.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      We keep order records for as long as tax law requires us to, and no longer for any
      other purpose.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Email</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      If you email us we keep the email, because that is what email is. We use it to
      answer you and to keep a record of support requests. Support runs on
      <a href={legal.helpCentre} rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">Intercom</a>, so
      Intercom processes those conversations on our behalf. We do not add you to a
      marketing list off the back of a support question.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Your rights</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      Depending on where you live you may have rights to access, correct, export or
      delete personal data we hold about you. In practice the only personal data we are
      likely to hold is correspondence you have sent us. Write to
      <a
        href={`mailto:${legal.privacyEmail}`}
        class="link"
        >{legal.privacyEmail}</a
      > and we will act on it.
    </p>

    <h2 class="font-serif text-h3 text-foreground mt-12">Changes</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-body text-muted-foreground text-pretty">
      If what the software does with data changes, this page changes with it and the date
      at the top moves. A change that widened what we collect would be announced, not
      slipped in.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
